# NO env file is committed, in any bucket. The scheme's buckets 1 and 2 -- .env.defaults and
# .env.<env> -- were tracked while this repository was private, on the correct grounds that they
# carry URLs and identities rather than passwords. Public changes the question: they name internal
# test accounts and the hostnames of QA deployments, which is not a secret and is not something to
# publish either. Bucket 3 was never committed and never will be.
#
# A clone therefore arrives with no deployment configuration, which is correct: this workbench
# describes whichever deployment you point it at. See .env.example for the keys it reads.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

node_modules/
_*.mjs

# Raw session logs. Per-run working data, and it carries the question text an agent typed and the
# targets it addressed -- so it goes through vendor/agent-log/scrub-scan.mjs before any of it is
# published, and the analysed result lands in measurements/ rather than here.
MEASUREMENT/
MEASUREMENT-archive/

# Local overrides that must never travel. settings.local.json was protected only by a rule in one
# developer's personal global ignore file (~/.config/git/ignore) -- protection that does not survive
# a clone, so the next person to have one would have had nothing stopping them committing it. It
# can carry env values: during the run-06 experiment it carried VC_MEASURE_OUT.
.claude/settings.local.json
*.local.json

# Per-run scratch. Run 07 created .scratch-run07/ for response bodies pulled off a live deployment;
# those are unredacted platform responses and belong nowhere near a commit.
#
# THE RULE ABOVE DID NOT COVER THE VERY NEXT RUN. Run 08 wrote order-67.json -- a GraphQL response
# body -- into the repository ROOT, where no scratch pattern reaches it, because nothing had ever
# told a run where scratch goes. A rule written for the shape of the last failure catches the last
# failure. So the brief now names .scratch/ and this covers it, and the loose shapes runs have
# actually produced are named too.
.scratch*/
*-[0-9][0-9].json
response-*.json
r[0-9]*.json
